{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Found while validating mechanical-coherent-wolverine's premise (23b2-1a94-6a01-466b).\n\n## Report\n\n`rebar._commands.txn.transition_core` declares `force_close_reason: str = \"\"` at\n`src/rebar/_commands/txn.py:198` and **never reads it**. The identifier occurs exactly once in\nthe whole file — the parameter declaration itself.\n\nThe caller does supply a real value: `src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py:474` passes\n`force_close_reason=force_close`, the string an operator typed into `--force=<reason>`. It is\naccepted, threaded one call deep into the locked write, and silently dropped before it reaches\nthe STATUS event.\n\n## Premise verified in current `origin/main`\n\n* `txn.py:198` — the parameter, declared with a default and never referenced again.\n* `transition_close.py:474` — the caller passing it.\n* `txn.py:277-296` — the close STATUS event `data`: `status`, `current_status`,\n  `parent_status_uuid`, and present-only `close_class`. No reason field of any kind.\n* `transition_close.py:511-521` — the ONLY durable trace of a force-close reason today is a\n  best-effort `FORCE_CLOSE` audit comment written AFTER the close, via a SECOND lock\n  acquisition, and swallowed on failure with only a `logger.warning`.\n\nThat last point is what makes this more than tidiness. Force-closing is most attractive\nprecisely when gates or the store are contended — and that is exactly when the second lock\nacquisition is most likely to time out and take the bypass reason with it, while the parameter\ndesigned to record it durably, in the write that already holds the lock, sits unused.\n\n## Direction\n\nWrite the reason into the close STATUS event, mirroring `close_class` — which is the exact\nsame shape of datum (a present-only, close-only string) and already has a complete, proven\npath through the system:\n\n| step | `close_class` (precedent) | `force_close_reason` (this change) |\n|---|---|---|\n| event write | `txn.py:287-288`, present-only so an absent value omits the key and stays byte-identical to a pre-feature event | same guard, same place |\n| reducer fold | `_fold_close_class`, called at BOTH `*->closed` edges (`reducer/_processors.py:203`, `:274` fork-winner, `:301` plain) | a sibling fold at the same two sites |\n| reduced state | declared in `schemas/ticket_state.schema.json:187` | declared alongside it |\n\nPresent-only matters for the same reason it did for `close_class`: a close with no `--force`\nomits the key entirely, so ordinary close events are unchanged byte-for-byte and an older\nreader that does not know the field is unaffected.\n\n**Alternative considered and rejected: delete the parameter as dead.** That is smaller and\nwould also satisfy \"no caller silently passes a discarded value\". It is rejected because the\ncaller has a genuinely useful value to record, the operator supplied it expressly to explain a\ngate bypass, and the existing audit-comment path is unreliable under exactly the conditions\nthat produce force-closes. Deleting would lock in the weaker of the two records.\n\nScope note: this does NOT add wolverine's `completion_expectation` enum, its derivation table,\nor any computation of gate state. This is one present-only string moved from a discarded\nparameter into the event that already carries `close_class`. The two tickets touch the same\nevent and should be sequenced, not merged.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A `--force=<reason>` close records that reason on the close STATUS event, written in the\n      SAME locked write as the close with no additional lock acquisition — verified by a test\n      reading the event\n- [ ] The reason is readable from reduced state by a later reader holding no lock — verified by\n      a test\n- [ ] A close WITHOUT `--force` omits the key entirely, leaving the event byte-identical to the\n      pre-change shape — verified by a test asserting absence\n- [ ] No caller passes a value that `transition_core` discards — verified by a test that would\n      fail if the parameter were reintroduced as unused"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786566848865445001,"uuid":"32c68186-81b6-4c4b-9c63-84fccf671b3b"}